WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:05.380
Another screening method, called
marker-assisted breeding, utilizes DNA

2
00:00:05.390 --> 00:00:10.940
based technology to rapidly determine
which offspring have desired genes. To

3
00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:15.130
better explain this, let's see an
example from Dr. Oliver Dong, a

4
00:00:15.130 --> 00:00:17.250
researcher in Dr. Ronald's lab.

5
00:00:18.490 --> 00:00:22.830
So in agriculture production, very
often, we like to combine good traits

6
00:00:22.830 --> 00:00:27.910
from different plants. In this case, we
may have a parental plant that sets a

7
00:00:27.910 --> 00:00:32.140
lot of seeds, and we have another
parent which does not set a lot of

8
00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:37.620
seeds, but has a lot of branches.
Basically, we cross the two parents,

9
00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:43.720
and we identify in the following
generations the progeny that has

10
00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:48.330
combined good traits from both sides.
In this case, it would be a plant that

11
00:00:48.610 --> 00:00:53.860
has lots of branches, and has lots of
seeds on each branch. However, there's

12
00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:59.300
a challenge with this approach. Think
of a huge population of thousands of

13
00:00:59.310 --> 00:01:05.440
plants. How can you determine which one
is the best one that contains the best

14
00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:09.770
traits? That's why we need a better
method, called marker-assisted

15
00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:15.230
breeding. Agricultural performance of
plants are usually linked to traits

16
00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:17.190
that are encoded by DNA.

17
00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:23.050
In this case, the dark circles
represent good genes, which will

18
00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:28.600
control a good trait, whereas the open
circles represent not so good genes,

19
00:01:28.610 --> 00:01:32.720
which will not give rise to a good
trait. In marker-assisted breeding,

20
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:37.720
instead of looking at the agricultural
performance directly in the offspring

21
00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:43.240
population, we look at the genetic
makeup of these plants, and we choose

22
00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:49.530
the individual that contains the most
dark circles, or the most good genes,

23
00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:55.100
because this individual will have the
best agricultural performance. And this

24
00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:59.630
method allows scientists to quickly
select the best individual in a

25
00:01:59.630 --> 00:02:04.868
relatively short period of time,
compared to traditional breeding. This

26
00:02:04.868 --> 00:02:09.208
is the method that Dr. David Mackill, a
rice breeder at UC Davis in the

27
00:02:09.208 --> 00:02:13.918
International Rice Research Institute,
used to develop the original Sub1 rice

28
00:02:13.918 --> 00:02:18.248
strain. First, a submergence tolerant
rice strain, FR13A,

29
00:02:19.818 --> 00:02:24.898
was crossed with a variety of Swarna
rice. Then the hybrid was crossed back

30
00:02:24.898 --> 00:02:29.158
to Swarna and selected over many
generations to clear the undesired

31
00:02:29.158 --> 00:02:30.678
genetic background from FR13A.

32
00:02:32.498 --> 00:02:37.268
What we're left with is a robustly
growing strain of Swarna rice that can

33
00:02:37.268 --> 00:02:39.538
withstand flooding for up to two weeks.

